{% comment %}
  Navigation fragment.

  This fragment emits two <ul> elements instead of one, and that difference is
  the entire layout. A <nav> in Pico is a flex row with justify-content:
  space-between, and the <ul> elements inside it are the groups that row
  distributes. The brand is already the first one, so the section links become
  the middle group and the account controls the last, which lands the site name
  at the left edge, the sections in the middle, and Sign in or Sign out at the
  right, with no CSS of our own and no utility classes.

  There is deliberately no collapse toggle here. Pico has no breakpoint at which
  a navbar hides itself and waits for a click to reappear, so the row just gets
  tighter until it wraps. That looks worse on a narrow phone than a burger menu
  does, and better than a menu whose links do not exist when scripting is turned
  off, which is the trade a JavaScript navbar makes. Keeping the navigation
  markup-only also keeps this theme free of any script at all.

  The items arrive already filtered: deciding whether a viewer may see a link
  belongs to the authorization layer, and a template that re-implemented it here
  would be a second answer to "what is WANT:ADMIN", one that disagrees with the
  first as soon as the rules grow a case, and disagrees in the direction of
  showing a link the route behind it then refuses.

  The active item comes from pageBase, which the framework derives from the
  request path, so a page cannot highlight the wrong thing by forgetting to say
  where it is. The active state is the aria-current attribute rather than a
  class, because that is what Pico's links key their active appearance off, and
  it is read by assistive technology as well as by the stylesheet.
{% endcomment %}
<ul>
  {% for item in navElements %}
  <li>
    {% if item.Children %}
    {%- comment %}
      A group with children becomes one of Pico's dropdowns, which are a
      <details> and a <summary> and therefore open on a click and close on
      another with no script behind them.
    {%- endcomment %}
    <details class="dropdown">
      <summary>{{ item.Label }}</summary>
      <ul>
        {% for child in item.Children %}
        <li><a href="{{ child.Href }}">{{ child.Label }}</a></li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    </details>
    {% else %}
    <a href="{{ item.Href }}"{% if item.Base == pageBase %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>
      {%- if item.Icon %}<span aria-hidden="true">{{ item.Icon }}</span> {% endif -%}
      {{ item.Label }}
    </a>
    {% endif %}
  </li>
  {% empty %}
  <li><small>No sections available</small></li>
  {% endfor %}
</ul>

<ul>
  {% if isAuthenticated %}
  <li>
    {%- comment %}
      Signing out is a POST, so it is a form with the CSRF token rather than a
      link, and a link that a prefetching crawler could follow by accident.
    {%- endcomment %}
    <form method="post" action="{{ signOutURL|default:'/logout' }}">
      {{ csrfInput }}
      <button type="submit" class="secondary">Sign out {{ user.Identity }}</button>
    </form>
  </li>
  {% else %}
  <li>
    <a href="{{ signInURL|default:'/login' }}" role="button" class="secondary">Sign in</a>
  </li>
  {% endif %}
</ul>
